WEBVTT

00:00.260 --> 00:02.500
When I'm marking practice answers,

00:02.980 --> 00:07.400
I often see two people describe
the same graph in very different ways.

00:07.940 --> 00:11.490
Here are two paragraphs about this electricity graph.

00:12.730 --> 00:15.690
Pause the video and decide
which one is stronger, and why.

00:28.270 --> 00:30.150
Paragraph B is stronger.

00:30.850 --> 00:32.930
Paragraph A lists every number,

00:33.380 --> 00:35.780
but it doesn't tell the reader what happened.

00:36.410 --> 00:42.780
Paragraph B picks out the key moments:
a crossover, a peak and a lowest point.

00:43.780 --> 00:46.300
So let's look at those.

00:46.300 --> 00:50.890
When two lines cross, describe it from the rising line:
gas overtook coal.

00:51.470 --> 00:54.640
Or from the falling line: coal fell below gas.

00:55.900 --> 01:01.250
And notice the time. The lines cross
somewhere between 2008 and 2012,

01:01.750 --> 01:04.600
so give the period, not a single year.

01:04.600 --> 01:08.940
Only use one year if the lines
meet exactly on a point.

01:10.220 --> 01:15.660
Then the turning points.
Gas peaked at 42% in 2012.

01:16.210 --> 01:20.410
Coal reached its lowest point, 12%, in 2020.

01:22.220 --> 01:27.090
Your turn. Look at renewables and gas.
Pause and write the sentence.

01:34.130 --> 01:40.560
Renewables overtook gas between 2016 and 2020,
reaching 46%.

01:41.980 --> 01:43.680
Now practise on the right page.
